# Scheduled jobs

Use a scheduled job for background work that belongs to a component. Declare the component, add an async `&self` method in a `#[jobs]` implementation, then install the jobs scheduler in the application.

## Schedule recurring work

<Example title="Refresh reports every ten minutes">

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::jobs::{JobProgress, JobRunContext, job, jobs};
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct ReportStore;

impl ReportStore {
    async fn refresh(&self) {}
}

#[component]
struct Reports;
```

Add the scheduled method. Ordinary parameters are resolved from the container for each run; `JobRunContext` comes from the scheduler.

```rust
#[jobs]
impl Reports {
    #[job(every = "10m", run_on_startup)]
    async fn refresh(&self, store: Arc<ReportStore>, cx: JobRunContext) {
        cx.progress(JobProgress::phase("refreshing")).await;
        store.refresh().await;
    }
}
```

</Example>

Install the jobs scheduler or plugin in application configuration so registered descriptors run. A job method must be async and use exactly `&self`; unlike protocol handlers, its dependencies are plain container-resolvable types, not `Inject<T>` wrappers.

## Return a failure the scheduler can record

Return `Result` when the scheduler needs to record a failed run.

<Example title="Record an upstream synchronization failure">

```rust context
#[component]
struct Reports;
```

```rust
struct SyncError;

impl std::fmt::Display for SyncError {
    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        formatter.write_str("upstream synchronization failed")
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for SyncError {}
```

Add the result-returning job to the same component.

```rust
#[jobs]
impl Reports {
    #[job(every = "15m")]
    async fn sync(&self) -> Result<(), SyncError> {
        Err(SyncError)
    }
}
```

</Example>

Any non-`Result` return is discarded after a successful completion. `Result::Err` must be a thread-safe standard error so the scheduler can retain the failure.

## Choose a cron policy for calendar work

Use `every` for elapsed intervals. Choose `cron` when the work must run on a calendar schedule, then add timeout, jitter, or overlap policy only for a concrete execution need.

<Example title="Reconcile once per hour without overlapping runs">

```rust context
#[component]
struct Reports;
```

```rust
#[jobs]
impl Reports {
    #[job(
        cron = "@hourly",
        timeout = "30s",
        overlap = CancelPrevious,
    )]
    async fn reconcile(&self) {
        // Reconcile report state.
    }
}
```

</Example>

Schedule strings are validated when the scheduler starts. Keep jobs in a separate inherent implementation from RPC or HTTP handlers so each collector macro owns its marked methods.

